  @.1652 text "]) provides greater independence for concurrent messages by using QUIC as a secure multiplexed transport over UDP instead of TCP.All three major versions of HTTP rely on the semantics defined by this document. They have not obsoleted each other because each one has specific benefits and limitations depending on the context of use. Implementations are expected to choose the most appropriate transport and messaging syntax for their particular context.This revision of HTTP separates the definition of semantics (this document) and caching (["
  @.1657 link "CACHING" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#CACHING"
  @.1659 text "]) from the current HTTP/1.1 messaging syntax (["
  @.1662 link "HTTP/1.1" url=$u1
  @.1664 text "]) to allow each major protocol version to progress independently while referring to the same core semantics."
  @.1666 link "1.3. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-1.3"
  @.1668 link "Core Semantics" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-core-semantics"
  @.1670 text "HTTP provides a uniform interface for interacting with a resource ("
  @.1671 link "Section 3.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#resources"
  @.1673 text ") -- regardless of its type, nature, or implementation -- by sending messages that manipulate or transfer representations ("
  @.1674 link "Section 3.2" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#representations"
  @.1676 text ").Each message is either a request or a response. A client constructs request messages that communicate its intentions and routes those messages toward an identified origin server. A server listens for requests, parses each message received, interprets the message semantics in relation to the identified target resource, and responds to that request with one or more response messages. The client examines received responses to see if its intentions were carried out, determining what to do next based on the status codes and content received.HTTP semantics include the intentions defined by each request method ("
  @.1679 link "Section 9" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#methods"
  @.1681 text "), extensions to those semantics that might be described in request header fields, status codes that describe the response ("
  @.1682 link "Section 15" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#status.codes"
  @.1684 text "), and other control data and resource metadata that might be given in response fields."
  @.1685 text "Semantics also include representation metadata that describe how content is intended to be interpreted by a recipient, request header fields that might influence content selection, and the various selection algorithms that are collectively referred to as "content negotiation" ("
  @.1686 link "Section 12" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#content.negotiation"
  @.1688 text ")."
  @.1689 link "1.4. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-1.4"
  @.1691 link "Specifications Obsoleted by This Document" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-specifications-obsoleted-by"
  @.1693 link "Table 1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#table-1"
  @.1695 text "TitleReferenceSeeHTTP Over TLS["
  @.1700 link "RFC2818" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#RFC2818"
  @.1702 text "]"
  @.1703 link "B.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#changes.from.rfc.2818"
  @.1705 text "HTTP/1.1 Message Syntax and Routing [*]["
  @.1707 link "RFC7230" url=$u2
  @.1709 text "]"
  @.1710 link "B.2" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#changes.from.rfc.7230"
  @.1712 text "HTTP/1.1 Semantics and Content["
  @.1714 link "RFC7231" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#RFC7231"
