  @.3535 text ")A sender MUST NOT send a connection option corresponding to a field that is intended for all recipients of the content. For example, Cache-Control is never appropriate as a connection option ("
  @.3539 link "Section 5.2" url="/rfc/rfc9111#section-5.2"
  @.3541 text " of ["
  @.3542 link "CACHING" url=$u1
  @.3544 text "]).Connection options do not always correspond to a field present in the message, since a connection-specific field might not be needed if there are no parameters associated with a connection option. In contrast, a connection-specific field received without a corresponding connection option usually indicates that the field has been improperly forwarded by an intermediary and ought to be ignored by the recipient."
  @.3547 text "When defining a new connection option that does not correspond to a field, specification authors ought to reserve the corresponding field name anyway in order to avoid later collisions. Such reserved field names are registered in the "Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Field Name Registry" ("
  @.3548 link "Section 16.3.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#fields.registry"
  @.3550 text ")."
  @.3551 link "7.6.2. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-7.6.2"
  @.3553 link "Max-Forwards" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-max-forwards"
  @.3555 text "The "Max-Forwards" header field provides a mechanism with the TRACE ("
  @.3556 link "Section 9.3.8" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#TRACE"
  @.3558 text ") and OPTIONS ("
  @.3559 link "Section 9.3.7" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#OPTIONS"
  @.3561 text ") request methods to limit the number of times that the request is forwarded by proxies. This can be useful when the client is attempting to trace a request that appears to be failing or looping mid-chain."
  @.3562 text "  Max-Forwards = 1*DIGIT
"
  @.3563 text "The Max-Forwards value is a decimal integer indicating the remaining number of times this request message can be forwarded.Each intermediary that receives a TRACE or OPTIONS request containing a Max-Forwards header field MUST check and update its value prior to forwarding the request. If the received value is zero (0), the intermediary MUST NOT forward the request; instead, the intermediary MUST respond as the final recipient. If the received Max-Forwards value is greater than zero, the intermediary MUST generate an updated Max-Forwards field in the forwarded message with a field value that is the lesser of a) the received value decremented by one (1) or b) the recipient's maximum supported value for Max-Forwards.A recipient MAY ignore a Max-Forwards header field received with any other request methods."
  @.3576 link "7.6.3. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-7.6.3"
  @.3578 link "Via" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-via"
  @.3580 text "The "Via" header field indicates the presence of intermediate protocols and recipients between the user agent and the server (on requests) or between the origin server and the client (on responses), similar to the "Received" header field in email ("
  @.3581 link "Section 3.6.7" url="/rfc/rfc5322#section-3.6.7"
  @.3583 text " of ["
  @.3584 link "RFC5322" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#RFC5322"
  @.3586 text "]). Via can be used for tracking message forwards, avoiding request loops, and identifying the protocol capabilities of senders along the request/response chain."
  @.3588 text "  Via = #( received-protocol RWS received-by [ RWS comment ] )

  received-protocol = [ protocol-name "/" ] protocol-version
                    ; see Section 7.8
  received-by       = pseudonym [ ":" port ]
  pseudonym         = token
"
  @.3589 text "Each member of the Via field value represents a proxy or gateway that has forwarded the message. Each intermediary appends its own information about how the message was received, such that the end result is ordered according to the sequence of forwarding recipients.A proxy MUST send an appropriate Via header field, as described below, in each message that it forwards. An HTTP-to-HTTP gateway MUST send an appropriate Via header field in each inbound request message and
