  @.8612 text ") to suggest an appropriate amount of time for the client to wait before retrying the request.Note: The existence of the 503 status code does not imply that a server has to use it when becoming overloaded. Some servers might simply refuse the connection."
  @.8615 link "15.6.5. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.6.5"
  @.8617 link "504 Gateway Timeout" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-504-gateway-timeout"
  @.8619 text "The 504 (Gateway Timeout) status code indicates that the server, while acting as a gateway or proxy, did not receive a timely response from an upstream server it needed to access in order to complete the request."
  @.8620 link "15.6.6. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.6.6"
  @.8622 link "505 HTTP Version Not Supported" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-505-http-version-not-suppor"
  @.8624 text "The 505 (HTTP Version Not Supported) status code indicates that the server does not support, or refuses to support, the major version of HTTP that was used in the request message. The server is indicating that it is unable or unwilling to complete the request using the same major version as the client, as described in "
  @.8625 link "Section 2.5" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#protocol.version"
  @.8627 text ", other than with this error message. The server SHOULD generate a representation for the 505 response that describes why that version is not supported and what other protocols are supported by that server."
  @.8630 link "16. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-16"
  @.8632 link "Extending HTTP" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-extending-http"
  @.8634 text "HTTP defines a number of generic extension points that can be used to introduce capabilities to the protocol without introducing a new version, including methods, status codes, field names, and further extensibility points within defined fields, such as authentication schemes and cache directives (see Cache-Control extensions in "
  @.8635 link "Section 5.2.3" url="/rfc/rfc9111#section-5.2.3"
  @.8637 text " of ["
  @.8638 link "CACHING" url=$u1
  @.8640 text "]). Because the semantics of HTTP are not versioned, these extension points are persistent; the version of the protocol in use does not affect their semantics.Version-independent extensions are discouraged from depending on or interacting with the specific version of the protocol in use. When this is unavoidable, careful consideration needs to be given to how the extension can interoperate across versions.Additionally, specific versions of HTTP might have their own extensibility points, such as transfer codings in HTTP/1.1 ("
  @.8644 link "Section 6.1" url="/rfc/rfc9112#section-6.1"
  @.8646 text " of ["
  @.8647 link "HTTP/1.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#HTTP11"
  @.8649 text "]) and HTTP/2 SETTINGS or frame types (["
  @.8652 link "HTTP/2" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#HTTP2"
  @.8654 text "]). These extension points are specific to the version of the protocol they occur within.Version-specific extensions cannot override or modify the semantics of a version-independent mechanism or extension point (like a method or header field) without explicitly being allowed by that protocol element. For example, the CONNECT method ("
  @.8657 link "Section 9.3.6" url=$u2
  @.8659 text ") allows this.These guidelines assure that the protocol operates correctly and predictably, even when parts of the path implement different versions of HTTP."
  @.8661 link "16.1. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-16.1"
  @.8663 link "Method Extensibility" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-method-extensibility"
  @.8665 link "16.1.1. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-16.1.1"
  @.8667 link "Method Registry" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-method-registry"
  @.8669 text "The "Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Method Registry", maintained by IANA at "
  @.8670 text "<"
  @.8671 link "https://www.iana.org/assignments/http-methods" url="https://www.iana.org/assignments/http-methods"
  @.8673 text ">, registers "
  @.8675 link "method" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#method.overview"
  @.8677 text " names.HTTP method registrations MUS
