  @.8402 link "413 Content Too Large" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-413-content-too-large"
  @.8404 text "The 413 (Content Too Large) status code indicates that the server is refusing to process a request because the request content is larger than the server is willing or able to process. The server MAY terminate the request, if the protocol version in use allows it; otherwise, the server MAY close the connection.If the condition is temporary, the server SHOULD generate a "
  @.8412 link "Retry-After" url=$u1
  @.8414 text " header field to indicate that it is temporary and after what time the client MAY try again."
  @.8417 link "15.5.15. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.5.15"
  @.8419 link "414 URI Too Long" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-414-uri-too-long"
  @.8421 text "The 414 (URI Too Long) status code indicates that the server is refusing to service the request because the target URI is longer than the server is willing to interpret. This rare condition is only likely to occur when a client has improperly converted a POST request to a GET request with long query information, when the client has descended into an infinite loop of redirection (e.g., a redirected URI prefix that points to a suffix of itself) or when the server is under attack by a client attempting to exploit potential security holes.A 414 response is heuristically cacheable; i.e., unless otherwise indicated by the method definition or explicit cache controls (see "
  @.8423 link "Section 4.2.2" url=$u2
  @.8425 text " of ["
  @.8426 link "CACHING" url=$u3
  @.8428 text "])."
  @.8430 link "15.5.16. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.5.16"
  @.8432 link "415 Unsupported Media Type" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-415-unsupported-media-type"
  @.8434 text "The 415 (Unsupported Media Type) status code indicates that the origin server is refusing to service the request because the content is in a format not supported by this method on the "
  @.8435 link "target resource" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#target.resource"
  @.8437 text ".The format problem might be due to the request's indicated "
  @.8439 link "Content-Type" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#field.content-type"
  @.8441 text " or "
  @.8442 link "Content-Encoding" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#field.content-encoding"
  @.8444 text ", or as a result of inspecting the data directly.If the problem was caused by an unsupported content coding, the "
  @.8446 link "Accept-Encoding" url=$u4
  @.8448 text " response header field ("
  @.8449 link "Section 12.5.3" url=$u4
  @.8451 text ") ought to be used to indicate which (if any) content codings would have been accepted in the request.On the other hand, if the cause was an unsupported media type, the "
  @.8453 link "Accept" url=$u5
  @.8455 text " response header field ("
  @.8456 link "Section 12.5.1" url=$u5
  @.8458 text ") can be used to indicate which media types would have been accepted in the request."
  @.8459 link "15.5.17. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.5.17"
  @.8461 link "416 Range Not Satisfiable" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-416-range-not-satisfiable"
  @.8463 text "The 416 (Range Not Satisfiable) status code indicates that the set of ranges in the request's "
  @.8464 link "Range" url=$u6
  @.8466 text " header field ("
  @.8467 link "Section 14.2" url=$u6
  @.8469 text ") has been rejected either because none of the requested ranges are satisfiable or because the client has requested an excessive number of small or overlapping ranges (a potential denial of service attack).Each range unit defines what is required for its own range sets to be satisfiable. For example, "
  @.8471 link "Section 14.1.2" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#byte.ranges"
