snapshot[522281-534265 of 696059]:
  @.8798 text "HTTP's most widely used extensibility point is the definition of new header and trailer fields.New fields can be defined such that, when they are understood by a recipient, they override or enhance the interpretation of previously defined fields, define preconditions on request evaluation, or refine the meaning of responses.However, defining a field doesn't guarantee its deployment or recognition by recipients. Most fields are designed with the expectation that a recipient can safely ignore (but forward downstream) any field not recognized. In other cases, the sender's ability to understand a given field might be indicated by its prior communication, perhaps in the protocol version or fields that it sent in prior messages, or its use of a specific media type. Likewise, direct inspection of support might be possible through an OPTIONS request or by interacting with a defined well-known URI ["
  @.8802 link "RFC8615" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#RFC8615"
  @.8804 text "] if such inspection is defined along with the field being introduced."
  @.8806 link "16.3.1. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-16.3.1"
  @.8808 link "Field Name Registry" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-field-name-registry"
  @.8810 text "The "Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Field Name Registry" defines the namespace for HTTP field names."
  @.8811 text "Any party can request registration of an HTTP field. See "
  @.8812 link "Section 16.3.2" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#considerations.for.new.fields"
  @.8814 text " for considerations to take into account when creating a new HTTP field."
  @.8815 text "The "Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Field Name Registry" is located at "
  @.8816 text "<"
  @.8817 link "https://www.iana.org/assignments/http-fields/" url="https://www.iana.org/assignments/http-fields/"
  @.8819 text ">"
  @.8820 text ". Registration requests can be made by following the instructions located there or by sending an email to the "ietf-http-wg@w3.org" mailing list."
  @.8821 text "Field names are registered on the advice of a designated expert (appointed by the IESG or their delegate). Fields with the status 'permanent' are Specification Required (["
  @.8823 link "RFC8126" url=$u1
  @.8825 text "], "
  @.8826 link "Section 4.6" url="/rfc/rfc8126#section-4.6"
  @.8828 text ").Registration requests consist of the following information:Field name:The requested field name. It MUST conform to the field-name syntax defined in "
  @.8834 link "Section 5.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#fields.names"
  @.8836 text ", and it SHOULD be restricted to just letters, digits, and hyphen ('-') characters, with the first character being a letter.Status:"
  @.8840 text ""permanent", "provisional", "deprecated", or "obsoleted"."
  @.8841 text "Specification document(s):Reference to the document that specifies the field, preferably including a URI that can be used to retrieve a copy of the document. Optional but encouraged for provisional registrations. An indication of the relevant section(s) can also be included, but is not required.And optionally:Comments:Additional information, such as about reserved entries.The expert(s) can define additional fields to be collected in the registry, in consultation with the community."
  @.8847 text "Standards-defined names have a status of "permanent". Other names can also be registered as permanent if the expert(s) finds that they are in use, in consultation with the community. Other names should be registered as "provisional"."
  @.8848 text "Provisional entries can be removed by the expert(s) if -- in consultation with the community -- the expert(s) find that they are not in use. The expert(s) can change a provisional entry's status to permanent at any time.Note that names can be registered by third parties (including the expert(s)) if the expert(s) determines that an unregistered name is widely deployed and not likely to be registered in a timely manner otherwise."
  @.8850 link "16.3.2. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-16.3.2"
  @.8852 link "Considerations for New Fields" url="/rfc/rf
... [tee capped at 4096 of 12014 bytes]

