snapshot[534266-546119 of 696059]:
  @.8975 link "Section 4.8" url=$u1
  @.8977 text ")."
  @.8978 link "16.4.2. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-16.4.2"
  @.8980 link "Considerations for New Authentication Schemes" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-considerations-for-new-auth"
  @.8982 text "There are certain aspects of the HTTP Authentication framework that put constraints on how new authentication schemes can work:HTTP authentication is presumed to be stateless: all of the information necessary to authenticate a request MUST be provided in the request, rather than be dependent on the server remembering prior requests. Authentication based on, or bound to, the underlying connection is outside the scope of this specification and inherently flawed unless steps are taken to ensure that the connection cannot be used by any party other than the authenticated user (see "
  @.8986 link "Section 3.3" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#connections"
  @.8988 text ")."
  @.8989 text "The authentication parameter "realm" is reserved for defining protection spaces as described in "
  @.8990 link "Section 11.5" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#protection.space"
  @.8992 text ". New schemes MUST NOT use it in a way incompatible with that definition."
  @.8995 text "The "token68" notation was introduced for compatibility with existing authentication schemes and can only be used once per challenge or credential. Thus, new schemes ought to use the auth-param syntax instead, because otherwise future extensions will be impossible."
  @.8996 text "The parsing of challenges and credentials is defined by this specification and cannot be modified by new authentication schemes. When the auth-param syntax is used, all parameters ought to support both token and quoted-string syntax, and syntactical constraints ought to be defined on the field value after parsing (i.e., quoted-string processing). This is necessary so that recipients can use a generic parser that applies to all authentication schemes.Note:"
  @.8998 text " The fact that the value syntax for the "realm" parameter is restricted to quoted-string was a bad design choice not to be repeated for new parameters."
  @.8999 text "Definitions of new schemes ought to define the treatment of unknown extension parameters. In general, a "must-ignore" rule is preferable to a "must-understand" rule, because otherwise it will be hard to introduce new parameters in the presence of legacy recipients. Furthermore, it's good to describe the policy for defining new parameters (such as "update the specification" or "use this registry")."
  @.9000 text "Authentication schemes need to document whether they are usable in origin-server authentication (i.e., using "
  @.9001 link "WWW-Authenticate" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#field.www-authenticate"
  @.9003 text "), and/or proxy authentication (i.e., using "
  @.9004 link "Proxy-Authenticate" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#field.proxy-authenticate"
  @.9006 text ").The credentials carried in an "
  @.9008 link "Authorization" url=$u2
  @.9010 text " header field are specific to the user agent and, therefore, have the same effect on HTTP caches as the "private" cache response directive ("
  @.9011 link "Section 5.2.2.7" url="/rfc/rfc9111#section-5.2.2.7"
  @.9013 text " of ["
  @.9014 link "CACHING" url=$u3
  @.9016 text "]), within the scope of the request in which they appear.Therefore, new authentication schemes that choose not to carry credentials in the "
  @.9019 link "Authorization" url=$u2
  @.9021 text " header field (e.g., using a newly defined header field) will need to explicitly disallow caching, by mandating the use of cache response directives (e.g., "private")."
  @.9022 text "Schemes using "
  @.9023 link "Authentication-Info" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#field.authentication-info"
  @.9025 text ", "
  @.9026 link "Proxy-Authentication-Info" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#field.proxy-authentication-info"
  @.9028 text ", or any other authentication related response header field need to consider and document the related security considerations (see "
  @.9029 link "Section 17.16.4" url="/rfc/rfc91
... [tee capped at 4096 of 11741 bytes]

