  @.11739 link "Section 2.2" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#requirements.notation"
  @.11741 text ")"
  @.11742 text "The description of an origin and authoritative access to origin servers has been extended for both "http" and "https" URIs to account for alternative services and secured connections that are not necessarily based on TCP. (Sections "
  @.11743 link "4.2.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#http.uri"
  @.11745 text ", "
  @.11746 link "4.2.2" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#https.uri"
  @.11748 text ", "
  @.11749 link "4.3.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#origin"
  @.11751 text ", and "
  @.11752 link "7.3.3" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#routing.origin"
  @.11754 text ")Explicit requirements have been added to check the target URI scheme's semantics and reject requests that don't meet any associated requirements. ("
  @.11756 link "Section 7.4" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#routing.reject"
  @.11758 text ")Parameters in media type, media range, and expectation can be empty via one or more trailing semicolons. ("
  @.11760 link "Section 5.6.6" url=$u1
  @.11762 text ")"
  @.11763 text ""Field value" now refers to the value after multiple field lines are combined with commas -- by far the most common use. To refer to a single header line's value, use "field line value". ("
  @.11764 link "Section 6.3" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#header.fields"
  @.11766 text ")Trailer field semantics now transcend the specifics of chunked transfer coding. The use of trailer fields has been further limited to allow generation as a trailer field only when the sender knows the field defines that usage and to allow merging into the header section only if the recipient knows the corresponding field definition permits and defines how to merge. In all other cases, implementations are encouraged either to store the trailer fields separately or to discard them instead of merging. ("
  @.11768 link "Section 6.5.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#trailers.limitations"
  @.11770 text ")The priority of the absolute form of the request URI over the Host header field by origin servers has been made explicit to align with proxy handling. ("
  @.11772 link "Section 7.2" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#field.host"
  @.11774 text ")"
  @.11775 text "The grammar definition for the Via field's "received-by" was expanded in RFC 7230 due to changes in the URI grammar for host "
  @.11776 text "["
  @.11777 link "URI" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#URI"
  @.11779 text "] that are not desirable for Via. For simplicity, we have removed uri-host from the received-by production because it can be encompassed by the existing grammar for pseudonym. In particular, this change removed comma from the allowed set of characters for a host name in received-by. ("
  @.11781 link "Section 7.6.3" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#field.via"
  @.11783 text ")"
  @.11784 link "B.3. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#appendix-B.3"
  @.11786 link "Changes from RFC 7231" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-changes-from-rfc-7231"
  @.11788 text "Minimum URI lengths to be supported by implementations are now recommended. ("
  @.11789 link "Section 4.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#uri.references"
  @.11791 text ")The following have been clarified: CR and NUL in field values are to be rejected or mapped to SP, and leading and trailing whitespace needs to be stripped from field values before they are consumed. ("
  @.11793 link "Section 5.5" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#fields.values"
  @.11795 text ")Parameters in media type, media range, and expectation can be empty via one or more trailing semicolons. ("
  @.11797 link "Section 5.6.6" url=$u1
  @.11799 text ")An abstract data type for HTTP messages has been introduced to define the components of a message and their semantics as an abstraction across multiple HTTP versions, rather than in terms of the specific syntax form of HTTP/1.1 in ["
  @.11802 link "HTTP/1.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#HTTP11"
  @.11804 text "], and reflect the contents after the message is parsed. This makes it easier to distinguish between requirements on the content (what is conveyed) versus requirements on the messaging syntax (ho
... [tee capped at 4096 of 11872 bytes]

