snapshot[82155-93675 of 696059]:
  @.2019 text "HTTP is a stateless request/response protocol for exchanging "messages" across a "
  @.2020 link "connection" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#connections"
  @.2022 text ". The terms "sender" and "recipient" refer to any implementation that sends or receives a given message, respectively."
  @.2023 text "A client sends requests to a server in the form of a "request" message with a method ("
  @.2024 link "Section 9" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#methods"
  @.2026 text ") and request target ("
  @.2027 link "Section 7.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#target.resource"
  @.2029 text "). The request might also contain header fields ("
  @.2030 link "Section 6.3" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#header.fields"
  @.2032 text ") for request modifiers, client information, and representation metadata, content ("
  @.2033 link "Section 6.4" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#content"
  @.2035 text ") intended for processing in accordance with the method, and trailer fields ("
  @.2036 link "Section 6.5" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#trailer.fields"
  @.2038 text ") to communicate information collected while sending the content."
  @.2039 text "A server responds to a client's request by sending one or more "response" messages, each including a status code ("
  @.2040 link "Section 15" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#status.codes"
  @.2042 text "). The response might also contain header fields for server information, resource metadata, and representation metadata, content to be interpreted in accordance with the status code, and trailer fields to communicate information collected while sending the content."
  @.2043 link "3.5. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-3.5"
  @.2045 link "User Agents" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-user-agents"
  @.2047 text "The term "user agent" refers to any of the various client programs that initiate a request."
  @.2048 text "The most familiar form of user agent is the general-purpose Web browser, but that's only a small percentage of implementations. Other common user agents include spiders (web-traversing robots), command-line tools, billboard screens, household appliances, scales, light bulbs, firmware update scripts, mobile apps, and communication devices in a multitude of shapes and sizes.Being a user agent does not imply that there is a human user directly interacting with the software agent at the time of a request. In many cases, a user agent is installed or configured to run in the background and save its results for later inspection (or save only a subset of those results that might be interesting or erroneous). Spiders, for example, are typically given a start URI and configured to follow certain behavior while crawling the Web as a hypertext graph.Many user agents cannot, or choose not to, make interactive suggestions to their user or provide adequate warning for security or privacy concerns. In the few cases where this specification requires reporting of errors to the user, it is acceptable for such reporting to only be observable in an error console or log file. Likewise, requirements that an automated action be confirmed by the user before proceeding might be met via advance configuration choices, run-time options, or simple avoidance of the unsafe action; confirmation does not imply any specific user interface or interruption of normal processing if the user has already made that choice."
  @.2051 link "3.6. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-3.6"
  @.2053 link "Origin Server" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-origin-server"
  @.2055 text "The term "origin server" refers to a program that can originate authoritative responses for a given target resource."
  @.2056 text "The most familiar form of origin server are large public websites. However, like user agents being equated with browsers, it is easy to be misled into thinking that all origin servers are alike. Common origin servers also include home automation units, configurable networking components, office machines, autonomous robots, news feeds, traffic cameras, real-time ad selectors, and video-on-demand platforms.Most HTTP communication consists of a retr
... [tee capped at 4096 of 11618 bytes]

