  @.3916 link "https://www.iana.org/assignments/character-sets" url="https://www.iana.org/assignments/character-sets"
  @.3918 text ">) according to the procedures defined in "
  @.3920 link "Section 2" url="/rfc/rfc2978#section-2"
  @.3922 text " of ["
  @.3923 link "RFC2978" url=$u2
  @.3925 text "].Note:"
  @.3928 text " In theory, charset names are defined by the "mime-charset" ABNF rule defined in "
  @.3929 link "Section 2.3" url="/rfc/rfc2978#section-2.3"
  @.3931 text " of ["
  @.3932 link "RFC2978" url=$u2
  @.3934 text "] (as corrected in ["
  @.3937 link "Err1912" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#Err1912"
  @.3939 text "]"
  @.3940 text "). That rule allows two characters that are not included in "token" ("{" and "}"), but no charset name registered at the time of this writing includes braces (see "
  @.3941 text "["
  @.3942 link "Err5433" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#Err5433"
  @.3944 text "])."
  @.3946 link "8.3.3. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-8.3.3"
  @.3948 link "Multipart Types" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-multipart-types"
  @.3950 text "MIME provides for a number of "multipart" types -- encapsulations of one or more representations within a single message body. All multipart types share a common syntax, as defined in "
  @.3951 link "Section 5.1.1" url="/rfc/rfc2046#section-5.1.1"
  @.3953 text " of ["
  @.3954 link "RFC2046" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#RFC2046"
  @.3956 text "], and include a boundary parameter as part of the media type value. The message body is itself a protocol element; a sender MUST generate only CRLF to represent line breaks between body parts."
  @.3960 text "HTTP message framing does not use the multipart boundary as an indicator of message body length, though it might be used by implementations that generate or process the content. For example, the "multipart/form-data" type is often used for carrying form data in a request, as described in "
  @.3961 text "["
  @.3962 link "RFC7578" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#RFC7578"
  @.3964 text "]"
  @.3965 text ", and the "multipart/byteranges" type is defined by this specification for use in some "
  @.3966 link "206 (Partial Content)" url=$u3
  @.3968 text " responses (see "
  @.3969 link "Section 15.3.7" url=$u3
  @.3971 text ")."
  @.3972 link "8.4. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-8.4"
  @.3974 link "Content-Encoding" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-content-encoding"
  @.3976 text "The "Content-Encoding" header field indicates what content codings have been applied to the representation, beyond those inherent in the media type, and thus what decoding mechanisms have to be applied in order to obtain data in the media type referenced by the "
  @.3977 link "Content-Type" url=$u4
  @.3979 text " header field. Content-Encoding is primarily used to allow a representation's data to be compressed without losing the identity of its underlying media type."
  @.3980 text "  Content-Encoding = #content-coding
"
  @.3981 text "An example of its use is"
  @.3982 text "Content-Encoding: gzip
"
  @.3983 text "If one or more encodings have been applied to a representation, the sender that applied the encodings MUST"
  @.3985 text " generate a Content-Encoding header field that lists the content codings in the order in which they were applied. Note that the coding named "identity" is reserved for its special role in "
  @.3986 link "Accept-Encoding" url=$u1
  @.3988 text " and thus SHOULD NOT be included.Additional information about the encoding parameters can be provided by other header fields not defined by this specification.Unlike Transfer-Encoding ("
