  @.4120 text "A language tag is a sequence of one or more case-insensitive subtags, each separated by a hyphen character ("-", %x2D). In most cases, a language tag consists of a primary language subtag that identifies a broad family of related languages (e.g., "en" = English), which is optionally followed by a series of subtags that refine or narrow that language's range (e.g., "en-CA" = the variety of English as communicated in Canada). Whitespace is not allowed within a language tag. Example tags include:"
  @.4121 text "  fr, en-US, es-419, az-Arab, x-pig-latin, man-Nkoo-GN
"
  @.4122 text "See ["
  @.4124 link "RFC5646" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#RFC5646"
  @.4126 text "] for further information."
  @.4128 link "8.6. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-8.6"
  @.4130 link "Content-Length" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-content-length"
  @.4132 text "The "Content-Length" header field indicates the associated representation's data length as a decimal non-negative integer number of octets. When transferring a representation as content, Content-Length refers specifically to the amount of data enclosed so that it can be used to delimit framing (e.g., "
  @.4133 link "Section 6.2" url="/rfc/rfc9112#section-6.2"
  @.4135 text " of ["
  @.4136 link "HTTP/1.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#HTTP11"
  @.4138 text "]). In other cases, Content-Length indicates the selected representation's current length, which can be used by recipients to estimate transfer time or to compare with previously stored representations."
  @.4140 text "  Content-Length = 1*DIGIT
"
  @.4141 text "An example is"
  @.4142 text "Content-Length: 3495
"
  @.4143 text "A user agent SHOULD send Content-Length in a request when the method defines a meaning for enclosed content and it is not sending Transfer-Encoding. For example, a user agent normally sends Content-Length in a POST request even when the value is 0 (indicating empty content). A user agent SHOULD NOT send a Content-Length header field when the request message does not contain content and the method semantics do not anticipate such data.A server MAY send a Content-Length header field in a response to a HEAD request ("
  @.4151 link "Section 9.3.2" url=$u1
  @.4153 text "); a server MUST NOT send Content-Length in such a response unless its field value equals the decimal number of octets that would have been sent in the content of a response if the same request had used the GET method.A server MAY send a Content-Length header field in a "
  @.4159 link "304 (Not Modified)" url=$u2
  @.4161 text " response to a conditional GET request ("
  @.4162 link "Section 15.4.5" url=$u2
  @.4164 text "); a server MUST NOT send Content-Length in such a response unless its field value equals the decimal number of octets that would have been sent in the content of a "
  @.4167 link "200 (OK)" url=$u3
  @.4169 text " response to the same request.A server MUST NOT send a Content-Length header field in any response with a status code of "
  @.4173 link "1xx (Informational)" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#status.1xx"
  @.4175 text " or "
  @.4176 link "204 (No Content)" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#status.204"
  @.4178 text ". A server MUST NOT send a Content-Length header field in any "
  @.4181 link "2xx (Successful)" url=$u4
  @.4183 text " response to a CONNECT request ("
  @.4184 link "Section 9.3.6" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#CONNECT"
  @.4186 text ").Aside from the cases defined above, in the absence of Transfer-Encoding, an origin server SHOULD send a Content-Length header field when the content size is known prior to sending the complete header section. This will allow downstream recipients to measure transfer progress, know when a received message is complete, and potentially reuse the connection for additional requests.Any Content-Length field value greater than or equal to zero is valid. Since there is no predefined limit to the length of content, a recipient MUST anticipate potentially large decimal numerals and prevent parsing errors due to integer conversion overflows or precision loss due to integer 
... [tee capped at 4096 of 11861 bytes]

