  @.7393 text ": The request contains bad syntax or cannot be fulfilled"
  @.7394 link "5xx (Server Error)" url=$u1
  @.7396 text ": The server failed to fulfill an apparently valid requestHTTP status codes are extensible. A client is not required to understand the meaning of all registered status codes, though such understanding is obviously desirable. However, a client MUST understand the class of any status code, as indicated by the first digit, and treat an unrecognized status code as being equivalent to the x00 status code of that class.For example, if a client receives an unrecognized status code of 471, it can see from the first digit that there was something wrong with its request and treat the response as if it had received a "
  @.7401 link "400 (Bad Request)" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#status.400"
  @.7403 text " status code. The response message will usually contain a representation that explains the status.Values outside the range 100..599 are invalid. Implementations often use three-digit integer values outside of that range (i.e., 600..999) for internal communication of non-HTTP status (e.g., library errors). A client that receives a response with an invalid status code SHOULD process the response as if it had a "
  @.7407 link "5xx (Server Error)" url=$u1
  @.7409 text " status code."
  @.7410 text "A single request can have multiple associated responses: zero or more "interim" (non-final) responses with status codes in the "informational" ("
  @.7411 link "1xx" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#status.1xx"
  @.7413 text ") range, followed by exactly one "final" response with a status code in one of the other ranges."
  @.7414 link "15.1. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.1"
  @.7416 link "Overview of Status Codes" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-overview-of-status-codes"
  @.7418 text "The status codes listed below are defined in this specification. The reason phrases listed here are only recommendations -- they can be replaced by local equivalents or left out altogether without affecting the protocol.Responses with status codes that are defined as heuristically cacheable (e.g., 200, 203, 204, 206, 300, 301, 308, 404, 405, 410, 414, and 501 in this specification) can be reused by a cache with heuristic expiration unless otherwise indicated by the method definition or explicit cache controls ["
  @.7421 link "CACHING" url=$u2
  @.7423 text "]; all other status codes are not heuristically cacheable."
  @.7425 text "Additional status codes, outside the scope of this specification, have been specified for use in HTTP. All such status codes ought to be registered within the "Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Status Code Registry", as described in "
  @.7426 link "Section 16.2" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#status.code.extensibility"
  @.7428 text "."
  @.7429 link "15.2. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-15.2"
  @.7431 link "Informational 1xx" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-informational-1xx"
  @.7433 text "The 1xx (Informational) class of status code indicates an interim response for communicating connection status or request progress prior to completing the requested action and sending a final response. Since HTTP/1.0 did not define any 1xx status codes, a server MUST NOT send a 1xx response to an HTTP/1.0 client.A 1xx response is terminated by the end of the header section; it cannot contain content or trailers.A client MUST be able to parse one or more 1xx responses received prior to a final response, even if the client does not expect one. A user agent MAY ignore unexpected 1xx responses.A proxy MUST"
  @.7444 text " forward 1xx responses unless the proxy itself reques
